              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "You know present soy, eres, es and completed-past fui, fuiste, fue. The open or background past of ser uses a different short stem: era."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: the singular ser row",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "person",
                "open or background past"
              ],
              "columns": 2,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "person: yo. open or background past: era.",
                "person: tú. open or background past: eras.",
                "person: él / ella / usted. open or background past: era."
              ]
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say era, eras, era. The first and third forms match, just as they did in the regular imperfect rows. ¿Era usted? asks “Was it you?” without adding a new noun. Plural persons wait."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The root, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Era continues Latin eram, an old form in the esse family. That is the family already connected to Spanish ser and English essence. The preterite fue comes from another historical set, so era / fue is a real contrast inside one modern verb."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
